---
// P673 — bespoke in-kernel reconstruction.
//
// The page IS the recreation: one centred column hung between hairlines, filling
// the viewport edge-to-edge exactly as the source frames itself. A rule opens the
// page, a three-part nav sits between it and a second rule (wordmark left, one
// word of information on the grid's seventh column, contact flush right), a
// display headline fills the column, a third rule closes it, and then the entry
// ledger — full-column plates on a twelve-column grid, repeated eight times down
// the page. The mechanic is live, not pictured: the room scrolls and the nav is a
// frosted backdrop-filter plane the ledger passes under. Promoting the room to the
// page hands its scroll to the document, which is what the source does — sticky +
// backdrop-filter IS the mechanic, so CSS carries it and there is no script.
// Plates are de-branded kernel specimens: we rebuild the room, not the client's
// artwork. The work declares no palette, so nothing grafts: the sheet rides the
// ambient kernel --paper/--ink and inverts coherently with the mode.
//
// Reference (internal, never shipped): refs/works/p673.jpg, 1600×1000, ground L=255.
// Measured with scripts/ref-geometry.py, then read by eye where the tool is blunt
// (it reported 4 rows but merged the nav's closing rule into the nav band, and
// merged the whole ledger into one 720px band). Converted x/1600*100 → cqw:
//
//   column        rules/ink/plate all x300→1300  → padding-inline 18.75% (62.5cqw)
//   rule 1        y32, x301→1297                 → padding-top 2cqw + nav border-top
//   nav ink       cap y43 → baseline y60         → capH 17px; Archivo capH .686
//                                                  → 24.8px = clamp(.82rem, 1.55cqw, 1.6rem)
//   nav band      rule y33 → rule y70 = 37px     → line-height 1.5, no padding-block
//                                                  (Archivo asc .878 → baseline lands y59.9)
//   nav parts     x301→448 / x808→922 / x1202→1297 → 12-col grid, spans 1/7 · 7/11 · 11/-1
//                                                  (col 7 opens at x810 — the source hangs
//                                                  "Information" on the grid, not on centre;
//                                                  space-between would put it 40px left)
//   headline      cap y98 → baseline y212        → capH 114px → 166px = clamp(2.2rem, 10.4cqw, 11rem)
//   headline set  rule y70 → cap y98 = 28px      → line-height 1, margin-top .15cqw
//   rule 3        y250, ink baseline y212        → margin-top .65cqw
//   plate         y257→977, 999×721              → aspect-ratio 1.386, margin-top .375cqw (6px)
//   the fold      plate bottom y977 of 1000      → the first plate clears the fold whole
//
// The 6px rule→plate gap is the only ledger spacing the reference exposes (the
// caption rail and every entry after the first sit below the fold), so it is used
// as the ledger's whole meter: entry gap and plate→caption gap alike. The headline
// fills the column exactly in the reference ("Writer's Retreat", ink x301→1299) —
// a fitted display line we deliberately do not chase, because our headline is the
// de-branded "P673": match the cap height, accept the width delta.
//
// Archived case-study copy (removed from DOM 2026-07-17):
//
//   The signature —
//   The whole site is one column hung between two hairlines: a rule opens the page, a
//   three-part nav sits on it — wordmark left, one word of information centre, contact right —
//   and a display headline fills the column edge to edge before the ledger begins. From there
//   the same twelve-column entry repeats down the page, plate after plate, while the nav
//   detaches and frosts, floating over everything that scrolls beneath it.
//
//   Study notes —
//   One column width decides everything: nav, headline, and every plate share the same two
//   vertical edges, so the eye never re-measures the page — it just descends. The hairlines
//   meter that descent, and the frost earns its keep structurally: the nav needs no border or
//   box to hold its plane, because the blurred ledger sliding underneath declares the layering
//   for it. Repetition reads as rhythm here, not monotony, because the grid never flinches.
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('works', 'p673');
const w = entry!.data;
const ENTRIES = 8;
---

<Base title={`${w.title} — *blue red + purple/`} chrome="work">
  <main class="site">
    <div class="site__col">
      <header class="site__nav">
        <span class="site__wordmark">{w.title}&trade; Study</span>
        <span class="site__info">Information</span>
        <span class="site__contact">Mail, IG &nearr;</span>
      </header>

      <p class="site__masthead">{w.title}</p>
      <hr class="site__rule" />

      {
        Array.from({ length: ENTRIES }, (_, i) => (
          <section class="entry">
            <figure class="entry__plate">
              <span class="entry__num font-mono">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            </figure>
            <p class="entry__row font-mono ttu tracked">
              <span class="entry__idx">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <span class="entry__label">Entry — specimen plate</span>
              <span class="entry__meta">12-col</span>
            </p>
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The rebuilt room, now the whole page: the column runs x300→1300 of the
     reference's 1600 — 18.75% margins — and the document's own scroll gives the
     frosted nav something to float over. The page is its own query container:
     the stage used to provide one, so cq* units silently resolve against the
     viewport without this. `container-type: inline-size` applies style +
     inline-size containment only — it creates no scroll container, so the nav's
     `position: sticky` still resolves against the viewport, and Lenis smooths the
     document natively rather than transforming it. Never set overflow here: that
     would make .site the sticky nav's scroll container and kill the mechanic. */
  .site {
    container-type: inline-size;
    min-height: 100dvh;
    background-color: var(--paper);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* padding-top 2cqw = the reference's 32px above the opening rule. */
  .site__col {
    padding: 2cqw 18.75% 5cqw;
  }

  /* Frosted plane — the live mechanic. Sticky against the document now that the
     room is the page; the ledger passes under it and the blur declares the layer.
     Both hairlines belong to the nav (reference y32 and y70), so they travel with
     it. The band is 37px on 24.8px type — line-height 1.5 lands that height AND
     the measured baseline with no padding, so no font-metric fudging. */
  .site__nav {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    gap: 0 2%;
    border-block: 1px solid var(--ink);
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 72%, transparent);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
    font-size: clamp(0.82rem, 1.55cqw, 1.6rem);
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }
  /* The nav rides the same twelve columns as the ledger — that shared grid is the
     sheet's whole thesis, and it is what puts "Information" at x808 rather than on
     the column's centre. */
  .site__wordmark {
    grid-column: 1 / 7;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .site__info {
    grid-column: 7 / 11;
  }
  .site__contact {
    grid-column: 11 / -1;
    text-align: right;
  }
  .site__info,
  .site__contact {
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }

  /* Headline fills the column the way the source's does — display face, solid
     leading, flush left to the shared edge. 10.4cqw = 166px at 1600, which is the
     reference's 114px cap height through Archivo's .686 cap ratio; the old 7.5rem
     cap was sized for the ~1000px stage and set it at two-thirds scale. */
  .site__masthead {
    margin: 0.15cqw 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: var(--wght-display, 700);
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 10.4cqw, 11rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display, -0.02em);
  }
  .site__rule {
    height: 1px;
    background-color: var(--ink);
    border: none;
    margin: 0.65cqw 0 0;
  }

  /* One entry = one pass of the twelve-column grid: plate spans all twelve, the
     caption row sits on the same tracks. Repeated eight times, all on the 6px
     meter the reference sets between its closing rule and the first plate. */
  .entry {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    gap: 0.375cqw 2%;
    margin-top: 0.375cqw;
  }
  .entry__plate {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin: 0;
    /* Reference plate y257→977 at the full column: 999 × 721. */
    aspect-ratio: 1.386;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    background-color: var(--paper-1, var(--paper));
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
  }
  .entry__num {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 7cqw, 7.5rem);
    color: var(--ink-3, var(--ink-2));
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  /* One plate carries the spot colour — "artwork carries colour". */
  .entry:nth-of-type(3) .entry__num {
    color: var(--spot, var(--ink));
  }

  .entry__row {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: subgrid;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(0.62rem, 1.1cqw, 1.15rem);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .entry__idx {
    grid-column: 1 / 3;
  }
  .entry__label {
    grid-column: 3 / 10;
  }
  .entry__meta {
    grid-column: 10 / -1;
    text-align: right;
  }

  /* Phone: the source column adapting, not a second design. The reference's 18.75%
     margins would leave a 244px column on a 390px screen, so the column widens and
     the nav's three clusters take fewer tracks each — same grid, same order. */
  @container (max-width: 40rem) {
    .site__col {
      padding-inline: 6%;
    }
    .site__wordmark {
      grid-column: 1 / 6;
    }
    .site__info {
      grid-column: 6 / 10;
    }
    .site__contact {
      grid-column: 10 / -1;
    }
  }
</style>
